RPi5 Network Driver Implementation Plan
=======================================

This document provides the detailed implementation plan to bring
the RPi5 Ethernet driver from skeleton to fully functional.

Current State (Completed)
-------------------------
- RP1 PCIe driver probes and attaches
- BAR0 mapped for peripheral access
- GENET driver skeleton attaches to RP1 bus
- MAC reset sequence implemented
- ifnet allocated with basic capabilities
- MII bus read/write methods implemented

Remaining Work
--------------

PHASE 2: RP1 Infrastructure (Prerequisites)
============================================

2A. MSI-X Interrupt Setup [rp1.c]
---------------------------------
The RP1 uses MSI-X for interrupt delivery. Each peripheral gets
its own interrupt vector.

Tasks:
1. Allocate MSI-X vectors (pci_alloc_msix)
   - RP1 supports 64 MSI-X vectors
   - Ethernet uses vector 6 (RP1_INT_ETH)

2. Map BAR2 for MSI-X configuration registers
   - Used for interrupt acknowledge (IACK)
   - Level-triggered interrupts need IACK

3. Set up interrupt domain
   - Route child interrupts through parent
   - Handle IACK for level-triggered sources

Code pattern:
    int msix_count = 64;
    pci_alloc_msix(dev, &msix_count);
    for (i = 0; i < msix_count; i++) {
        sc->msix_irq[i] = bus_alloc_resource_any(dev, SYS_RES_IRQ,
            &i, RF_ACTIVE);
    }

2B. Child Device Enumeration [rp1.c]
------------------------------------
The RP1 parent driver needs to create child devices for each
peripheral block.

Tasks:
1. Add device_add_child() for Ethernet
   - Pass offset and size via ivars
   - Child probes based on presence check

2. Implement bus_read_ivar/bus_write_ivar
   - Pass configuration to children

3. Implement interrupt routing
   - bus_setup_intr routes to parent MSI-X
   - bus_teardown_intr cleans up

Code pattern:
    child = device_add_child(dev, "rp1eth", -1);
    device_set_ivars(child, &eth_ivars);
    bus_generic_attach(dev);


PHASE 3: DMA Infrastructure [rp1_eth.c]
=======================================

3A. DMA Tag Creation
--------------------
Create DMA tags for TX and RX buffers.

Tasks:
1. Create parent DMA tag from PCI device
   - Note: Parent is RP1, which is a PCIe device
   - Need to get DMA tag from grandparent

2. Create TX buffer tag
   - Alignment: 4 bytes
   - Max address: 40-bit (BUS_SPACE_MAXADDR_40BIT)
   - Max segments: 20 (TX_MAX_SEGS)
   - Max size: MCLBYTES

3. Create RX buffer tag
   - Same limits, but single segment

4. Create DMA maps for each ring entry
   - TX_DESC_COUNT maps for TX
   - RX_DESC_COUNT maps for RX

Code to add to rp1_eth_softc:
    bus_dma_tag_t       tx_buf_tag;
    bus_dma_tag_t       rx_buf_tag;

3B. TX/RX Ring Initialization
-----------------------------
Initialize the hardware descriptor rings.

Tasks:
1. Implement rp1_eth_init_txring()
   - Reset ring pointers
   - Configure ring buffer size
   - Enable TX DMA for queue

2. Implement rp1_eth_init_rxring()
   - Reset ring pointers
   - Configure ring buffer size
   - Pre-allocate RX buffers
   - Enable RX DMA for queue

3. Implement rp1_eth_newbuf_rx()
   - Allocate mbuf cluster
   - Map for DMA
   - Write descriptor address

Ring structure:
    struct tx_queue {
        int             hwindex;    // Hardware queue (16 = default)
        int             nentries;
        u_int           queued;
        u_int           cur;
        u_int           next;
        u_int           prod_idx;
        u_int           cons_idx;
        struct rp1_eth_ring_ent *entries;
    };


PHASE 4: Data Path [rp1_eth.c]
==============================

4A. Interrupt Handler
---------------------
Implement the main interrupt handler.

Tasks:
1. Implement rp1_eth_intr()
   - Read GENET_INTRL2_CPU_STAT
   - Mask processed interrupts
   - Call TX/RX handlers as needed
   - Clear interrupt status

2. Register interrupt with parent RP1
   - Request IRQ from parent (vector 6)
   - Set up handler

Code pattern:
    static void
    rp1_eth_intr(void *arg)
    {
        struct rp1_eth_softc *sc = arg;
        uint32_t status;

        RP1_ETH_LOCK(sc);
        status = RD4(sc, GENET_INTRL2_CPU_STAT);
        status &= ~RD4(sc, GENET_INTRL2_CPU_STAT_MASK);
        WR4(sc, GENET_INTRL2_CPU_CLEAR, status);

        if (status & GENET_IRQ_RXDMA_DONE)
            rp1_eth_rxintr(sc);
        if (status & GENET_IRQ_TXDMA_DONE)
            rp1_eth_txintr(sc);

        RP1_ETH_UNLOCK(sc);
    }

4B. TX Path
-----------
Implement packet transmission.

Tasks:
1. Implement rp1_eth_encap()
   - Load mbuf into DMA map
   - Handle fragmented mbufs (m_collapse if needed)
   - Write TX descriptors
   - Update producer index

2. Implement rp1_eth_start() fully
   - Dequeue packets from ifq
   - Call encap for each
   - Handle queue full condition

3. Implement rp1_eth_txintr()
   - Read consumer index
   - Free completed mbufs
   - Update statistics
   - Clear OACTIVE if queue drained

4. Implement checksum offload
   - Prepend status block for TX csum
   - Parse headers for offset calculation

4C. RX Path
-----------
Implement packet reception.

Tasks:
1. Implement rp1_eth_rxintr()
   - Read producer index
   - Process received packets
   - Check status for errors
   - Pass good packets to if_input

2. Implement buffer recycling
   - Allocate new buffer
   - Reuse old buffer on allocation failure

3. Implement checksum verification
   - Check status block for csum OK
   - Set mbuf flags accordingly


PHASE 5: PHY Management [rp1_eth.c]
===================================

Tasks:
1. Attach MII bus
   - Call mii_attach() in attach
   - PHY is at address 1 (BCM54213)
   - Use RGMII mode

2. Implement rp1_eth_miibus_statchg()
   - Update MAC speed/duplex
   - Configure RGMII OOB control
   - Handle link up/down

3. Implement rp1_eth_media_status()
   - Report current media state

4. Implement rp1_eth_media_change()
   - Trigger PHY renegotiation

5. Implement rp1_eth_tick()
   - Periodic PHY polling
   - Call mii_tick()

Code pattern:
    error = mii_attach(dev, &sc->miibus, sc->ifp,
        rp1_eth_media_change, rp1_eth_media_status,
        BMSR_DEFCAPMASK, MII_PHY_ANY, MII_OFFSET_ANY,
        MIIF_DOPAUSE);


PHASE 6: Full Initialization [rp1_eth.c]
========================================

Tasks:
1. Complete rp1_eth_init_locked()
   - Set port control for external GPHY
   - Configure MAC address
   - Setup RX filter
   - Initialize TX/RX rings
   - Enable MAC TX/RX
   - Enable interrupts
   - Start link timer

2. Complete rp1_eth_stop()
   - Disable interrupts
   - Stop TX/RX DMA
   - Drain rings
   - Release buffers

3. Implement rp1_eth_setup_rxfilter()
   - Configure multicast filter
   - Handle promiscuous mode

4. Implement rp1_eth_set_enaddr()
   - Program MAC address registers

5. MAC address source
   - Read from hardware (if preserved)
   - Read from DTB (if available)
   - Generate from hostid (fallback)


Testing Milestones
==================

After Phase 2:
- RP1 shows child devices in devinfo
- Interrupts can be allocated

After Phase 3:
- DMA rings allocate successfully
- No memory leaks on unload

After Phase 4:
- ping works (basic connectivity)
- iperf shows reasonable throughput

After Phase 5:
- Link detection works
- Speed/duplex autoneg works

After Phase 6:
- Full functionality
- Stress testing passes
- No panics or leaks


Implementation Order
====================

Recommended order to minimize debugging:

1. Phase 3A (DMA tags) - can test allocation/free
2. Phase 3B (Ring init) - can verify register writes
3. Phase 2A (MSI-X) - needed before interrupts work
4. Phase 2B (Child enum) - connect interrupt to child
5. Phase 5 (PHY) - get link up first
6. Phase 4A (Interrupt) - verify interrupt delivery
7. Phase 4C (RX) - easier to debug than TX
8. Phase 4B (TX) - complete with RX working
9. Phase 6 (Full init) - tie everything together


